Daemons: a light on the row, and a live check behind it
=====================================================

A page for the background services this app leans on, under Cron jobs, with a little horned face for an
icon.

The important part is that nothing on it is asserted. `/api/quixi/daemons` asks the machine:

  * `systemctl --user is-active <unit>` for each known service
  * a 120 ms TCP connect to the port it would be listening on

A row is lit only when the thing is actually reachable. The list names what each one is for rather than
just its unit name: streaming speech for dictation, Kokoro for read-aloud, the embedding index for
memory recall, the model server, vision, and input injection for the agent driving mouse and keyboard.

Missing services report as down rather than being hidden, because "this box cannot do voice" is
information the user needs, and a page that silently omits it makes the absence look like a bug.
diff -Naur /tmp/mk35/a39/crates/quixi-engine/src/lib.rs /tmp/mk35/b/crates/quixi-engine/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/quixi-engine/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/quixi-engine/src/lib.rs
@@ -1593,6 +1593,46 @@
         if clean_path == "/api/quixi/storage/status" {
             return Self::json_resp(200, &self.session_storage_status());
         }
+        // Daemons: what is running on this machine right now, by systemd unit and by listening port.
+        // Answering "is it up" from a live check beats a settings page that says a service "exists".
+        if clean_path == "/api/quixi/daemons" {
+            let unit_state = |unit: &str| -> String {
+                std::process::Command::new("systemctl")
+                    .args(["--user", "is-active", unit])
+                    .output()
+                    .map(|o| String::from_utf8_lossy(&o.stdout).trim().to_string())
+                    .unwrap_or_else(|_| "unknown".into())
+            };
+            let port_open = |port: u16| -> bool {
+                std::net::TcpStream::connect_timeout(
+                    &std::net::SocketAddr::from(([127, 0, 0, 1], port)),
+                    std::time::Duration::from_millis(120),
+                )
+                .is_ok()
+            };
+            let mut list: Vec<Value> = Vec::new();
+            for (name, unit, port, what) in [
+                ("Streaming speech", "nora-talk.service", Some(8791u16), "dictation and transcription"),
+                ("Kokoro voice", "kokoro.service", Some(8765u16), "read aloud"),
+                ("Embedding index", "embeddinggemma.service", Some(42666u16), "memory recall"),
+                ("Model server", "llama-swap.service", Some(8086u16), "local models"),
+                ("Vision", "qwen3vl.service", Some(8100u16), "looking at images"),
+                ("Input injection", "ydotoold", None, "the agent driving mouse and keyboard"),
+            ] {
+                let state = if unit.is_empty() { "n/a".to_string() } else { unit_state(unit) };
+                let listening = port.map(port_open);
+                list.push(json!({
+                    "name": name,
+                    "unit": unit,
+                    "what": what,
+                    "state": state,
+                    "port": port,
+                    "listening": listening,
+                    "up": listening.unwrap_or(state == "active"),
+                }));
+            }
+            return Self::json_resp(200, &json!({ "daemons": list }));
+        }
         // Where the data actually is, in paths rather than reassurance. The Privacy page shows this
         // instead of telling people their conversations are "stored on this device" — they can read
         // the directory, see the files, and open it in a file manager.
